[ChapterName "Vancura Position"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/heQDnvq7/YAqTqI8s"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lichess"]
[FEN "R7/6k1/P4r2/3K4/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.06"]
[UTCTime "15:49:17"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ The Vančura position is not a very common ending. It was studied by Josef Vančura and his analysis was published in 1924. The key idea is to keep giving lateral checks to drive White's king away from the queenside until white pushes the pawn to a7, then to play Ra6 to reach another well-known drawn rook ending.

Note that in the Vančura position, we can't try to do checks from the 1st rank because white's king can hide on a7 and then white will be able to promote the pawn with the help of the rook.

In this position, we can start the sequence of checks with Rf5. }
1... Rf5+ (1... Rb6 { Also leads to a draw, but play Rf5 to continue the exercise. }) (1... Kh7 { Also leads to a draw, but play Rf5 to continue the exercise. }) 2. Kc6 { White's king is getting closer to help the pawn promote, but it doesn't have any shelter on the queenside for the moment so we can just chase it away. } 2... Rf6+ 3. Kb7 Rf7+ 4. Kb6 Rf6+ 5. Kc5 { We can continue with the same idea. } 5... Rf5+ { If white's king gets back to the queenside, we can just continue our checks and white would not make any progress. } (5... Kh7 { Also leads to a draw, but play Rf5 to continue the exercise. }) 6. Kd4 Rf6 (6... Rf4+ { This position is deceptively tricky. While Rf6 in general is where we want the rook in the Vancura, in this position it is actually forced because if Black plays 6...Rf4+ then white will have the amazing resource 7.Ke5 Rf6 8.Rg8+! simplifying into a winning pawn endgame. }) 7. a7 { Now we can bring the rook to a6 since white pushed the pawn to a7. } 7... Ra6 { And we have reached a well-known drawn rook endgame. } *


