[Event "Lichess Practice: Intermediate Rook Endings: Holding Against a Rook Pawn"]
[Date "2022.07.04"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Lichess Practice: Intermediate Rook Endings"]
[ChapterName "Holding Against a Rook Pawn"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/heQDnvq7/4vQVBOaV"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lichess"]
[FEN "K7/P3k3/8/8/8/8/3R4/1r6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.04"]
[UTCTime "02:41:04"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ For rook endings with a rook pawn, as long as the king is 3 files away from the enemy pawn the ending is holdable because Black's king can make it in time to the crucial c7 square when white tries to bring the rook to the b-file to make a bridge. It is important to keep white's king obstructing the pawn with our rook on the b-file.

Move the rook to b3 to continue the exercise. } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gb1b3] }
1... Rb3 2. Rd1 { White hasn't made any progress, so we can keep moving our rook on the b-file. Play Rb2. } { [%cal Gb3b2] } 2... Rb2 3. Rh1 { Now white is trying to get to the b-file with Rh8-Rb8. Play Kd7 to bring the king to c7 on time. } { [%cal Ge7d7,Bh1h8,Bh8b8] } 3... Kd7 4. Rh8 Kc7 5. Rb8 { White brought the rook to the b-file, but the king is still obstructing the pawn. Exchanging the rooks here is obviously losing because white can recapture while promoting the pawn. Therefore we need to move our rook to any other file (except the a- and c-file). } { [%cal Gb2h2] } 5... Rh2 6. Rb7+ { Now we need to move our king in a way that white's king will not be able to leave the promotion square to the pawn. Note here that white will not be able to move the king if we move to the d6 or c6 square because of checkmate. } { [%cal Gc7d6,Gc7c6,Gc7c8,Rh2h8] } *


[Event "Lichess Practice: Intermediate Rook Endings: Advanced Philidor"]
[Date "2022.07.04"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Lichess Practice: Intermediate Rook Endings"]
[ChapterName "Advanced Philidor"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/heQDnvq7/WGNZ2n0k"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lichess"]
[FEN "4k3/7R/8/3KPr2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.04"]
[UTCTime "02:59:49"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ At first, this might look lost, as 1...Rg5 loses immediately to 2.Kd6. However, there is a way to hold this, with the Philidor idea of using the rook from behind. Bring the rook to f2 to continue the exercise. } { [%cal Gf5f2] }
1... Rf2 { Now we're getting ready to use the rook from behind to stop the pawn. Obviously Rf1 and Rf3 would have worked as well since it's the same idea. } 2. Kd6 { In this position, we can't play Rd2+ since white can go to e6 and it would be hard to stop the pawn.
Also note that white is preparing Rh8+ followed by e6+ – e7, and the pawn would be unstoppable. Additionally, if after Rh8+ we get back the rook to f8 white can simply trade and play Kd7, which would again make the pawn unstoppable.
What is the only move that stops white's threat? } 2... Re2 { With this move, Rh8+ followed by e6+ is useless since the rook is now guarding the e6 square. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Be8f7,Gf7e6,Ge2e6,Yh7h8] } 3. Rh8+ (3. Ke6 Kf8 4. Kf6 Rf2+ 5. Ke6 Re2 6. Rh8+ Kg7 7. Rh1 Kf8) 3... Kf7 4. Rh7+ { [%cal Gf7g6] } 4... Kg6 5. Rh1 { White realizes that the kingside is too short so they try to reroute the rook to a7. } { [%cal Gg6f7] } 5... Kf7 6. Rf1+ { Kg8 loses due to e6. } { [%cal Gf7e8] } 6... Ke8 7. Ra1 { [%cal Ge8f7] } 7... Kf7 8. Ra7+ { [%cal Gf7e8] } 8... Ke8 9. Ke6 { White is threatening checkmate. } { [%cal Ge8d8] } 9... Kd8 10. Ra8+ Kc7 11. Ra5 { Now we need to get back to our important squares before white prevents us from doing so. } { [%csl Bf8,Be8,Bd8] } 11... Kd8 12. Kd6 { [%cal Ge2d2] } 12... Rd2+ 13. Ke6 { [%cal Gd2e2] } 13... Re2 { White hasn't made any progress and the game will eventually end in a draw. } *


