1. c7 Rd6+ 2. Kb5 Rd5+ 3. Kb4 Rd4+ 4. Kc3 Rd1 5. Kc2 Rd4 { Kb3 and Kc3 work here as well here but it would transfer to the same position. } 6. c8=R Ra4 7. Kb3 { White is winning. } *


[Event "Lichess Practice: Intermediate Rook Endings: A Tricky Resource"]
[Date "2022.07.04"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Lichess Practice: Intermediate Rook Endings"]
[ChapterName "A Tricky Resource"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/heQDnvq7/pUZlG36u"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lichess"]
[FEN "8/8/2R5/4K1k1/8/pp6/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.04"]
[UTCTime "03:07:55"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ At first glance, it looks like White may have lost this game, as Black's pawns have made it all the way to the 6th rank. However, there is one last drawing trick. }
1. Rc1! { White tries to setup a mating net. } 1... a2 2. Rg1+ Kh4 3. Kf4 Kh3 4. Kf3 (4. Rh1+ { Also draws, but play Kf3 to continue with the same idea of keeping threatening checks. } 4... Kg2 5. Ra1 Kf2 6. Ke4 Ke2 7. Kd4 Kd2 8. Kc4 Kc2 9. Kb4 Kb2 10. Rh1 { and White can sacrifice the rook to win both pawns. }) 4... Kh4 { Black needs to keep moving the king to not be mated. Kh2 wouldn't have worked due to Ra1 and Black would have been in zugzwang. } (4... Kh2? 5. Ra1 { and Black is in zugzwang. }) 5. Kf4 Kh5 { Black needs to keep preventing checkmate on h1, the game will eventually end in a draw. } *


